// CPITEMBIOTIC_START — 🪲 THE BIOTIC INDEX WORKSHEET: the second real skill item type, the full `sheet` (DESIGN_class_platform_2026-10-28.md
// §4.1b, §6.7, §13 rows 25–27; RESEARCH_envirothon_skills_2026-10-28.md §4.3, ranked #2 in §5: "one chart drives several linked answers";
// her yes to building the design 2026-10-28; built 2026-10-29). The station hands over what was found in a stream and asks for the chart:
// the number of KINDS in each group × 3, 2, 1, the total, and the rating (Excellent 23+, Good 17–22, Fair 11–16, Poor 0–10). Area 8 asks it
// as linked questions: 2022 Aquatics Q17–20 (ID the snail in the picture, then "you also find 3 Group 1, 5 Group 2 and 1 Group 3", fill the
// chart, the rating) and 2026 Aquatics Q13–15 (a list of seven kinds, fill the chart, the rating). The cards have the multipliers and the
// ranges; the traps are STEPS: count kinds, not animals · put each one in its group · add in the one from the picture question. INERT off
// the site (cpskOn): Howe, DeWalt, Burris and Lincoln never reach a line of it.
//   · THE DATA is the unit's: units/<unitId>/unit.json `skills`, compiled by units/build/compile_unit.js (rule 8) into the catalog entry
//     (CPU_UNITS). A count-and-weigh worksheet is type "sheet" with data.kind "tally" and a flat items list: groups [{key, label, short,
//     weight}], items [{name, group, src, like:[look-alike names]}], ratings [{min, label, choice}], pick / chartPick (kinds in a sample),
//     most (animals of one kind), forms ("list" = 2026's, "chart" = 2022's). NC Envirothon: the 29 kinds of the NC "Guide to Macroinvertebrate
//     Taxa Groups and Aquatic Pollution Tolerance" (the guide Area 8's 2026 key cites; the same 29 as the "· pollution tolerance group"
//     cards), × 3 / 2 / 1 and the ranges of the Aquatics Manual p.62, the rating choices worded as Area 8 prints them. Nothing in the shape
//     is Envirothon-specific: basal area, tree height or unit price is the same sheet with other groups.
//   · EVERY TRY IS A NEW STREAM. A sitting deals its tries a rating each from a shuffled order (all four ratings come up), alternates the
//     two forms (so the two worked examples show both), and never deals the same counts twice. List form: 5–9 kinds, some seen more than
//     once, one of them shown ONLY as a picture → name it, sort every kind, # found per group, the index, the rating. Chart form: the
//     counts per group are given and one more kind is in the picture → name it, its group, # found per group (that one added), the index,
//     the rating. Through CPSKILL's three stages: 👀 all steps lit in turn · ✍️ the sort and the counts are done, the student does the rating,
//     then the index too · 💪 every step alone, the guide behind "Show the chart".
//   · THE PICTURES come from CPPHOTOS' pool only: cpPhotos(item, data.tags) — repo ← unit ← class, main first (else any photo of the item that
//     isn't a track or skull). Every kind in the sample shows its picture; a kind with none shows a "📷" placeholder, can't be the picture
//     question, and is listed: the teacher's 🏫 Class setup ▸ 🧩 Skills note (CPSKILL's type note) and compile_unit.js's note. Nothing is
//     hot-linked here, nothing added to the repo. A picture's credit waits until its name is out (it may hold the name).
//   · WHICH STEP WAS MISSED: id:<kind> (named the picture wrong) · group:<kind> (sorted it wrong) · count:individuals (counted animals, not
