// CPITEMSCALE_START — 📏 READ THE BILTMORE STICK: the `scale` skill item type (DESIGN_class_platform_2026-10-28.md §4.1b, §13 rows 25–27;
// RESEARCH_envirothon_skills_2026-10-28.md §4.9 "Forestry measurement", ranked #9 in §5 — "Field day and printables do more. Worth more at
// State or HS." — so it stays to what §4.9 gives the `scale` type: "A tree … with a Biltmore stick drawn on it. The kid reads the diameter
// class or counts logs"; §8.1 the three cards it can replace; her yes to building the design 2026-10-28; built 2026-10-29). The research:
// Area 8 MS asks mostly facts ABOUT the tools — DBH is at 4½ ft (2021 Q2), what a Biltmore stick measures (2021 Q10), the tool by name —
// and "a card can say 'hold it 25 inches away'. It can't make you read the scale." INERT off the site (cpskOn): Howe, DeWalt, Burris and
// Lincoln never reach a line of it.
//   · THE DATA is the unit's: units/<unitId>/unit.json `skills`, type "scale", data.kind "biltmore" — reach (inches from the eye, 25),
//     chain (feet from the tree, 66), log (feet, 16), and reads [{key "diameter"|"logs", label, unit, min, max, mark (the stick's marks:
//     1 in, ½ log), tol (how far from the true edge a reading still counts), ask:[{id, label, answer, wrong:[3], src, miss}], readLbl,
//     feetLbl}] — the questions and their words are data; compile_unit.js rule 8 checks them (and that every src is a real card).
//   · THE FORMULAS, exactly as the research gives them (§4.9 #1, and the printed-stick note in §6): a diameter mark sits
//     S = √(a·D² / (a + D)) inches from the stick's 0, a = 25 in (a 10-in tree reads at about 8.5 in) · a log mark: similar triangles,
//     16 ft seen from 66 ft on a stick 25 in from the eye = 16 × 25 ÷ 66 ≈ 6.1 in per log · height = logs × 16 + the partial log (a half
//     log is 8 ft; Forestry · Facts #o448). The drawing is MADE from these, so every tree size can be generated.
//   · A TRY: a NEW tree each time, in the research's order — a sitting alternates the diameter and the height (diameter first, unless she
//     missed the height more). Three steps each:
//       diameter (DBH): 1. how high on the trunk (4½ ft, breast height) · 2. how far from the eye (25 in) · 3. read the stick at the
//         trunk's right edge, 0 on its left edge — a tree of 6–24 in, its edge within 0.7 × tol of a whole-inch mark;
//       height: 1. how far to stand (66 ft, one chain) · 2. read the log mark at the top of the usable trunk (a fork or an 8-in top,
//         Forestry · Facts #o440), 0 at the stump — 1 to 4 logs by halves, the top within 0.7 × tol of a half-log mark · 3. the feet.
//     THE TOLERANCE is the tool's: a reading counts within tol of the true edge — ½ in (the nearest whole-inch mark) for the diameter,
//     ¼ log (the nearest half log) for the height — and the edge is drawn closer than that to one mark, so exactly one mark counts.
//     👀 Watch it: each step lit, the reading marked on the stick · ✍️ Finish it: the stick read for her, she finds the feet (height)
//     or reads it (diameter), then one more step each right round · 💪 On your own.
//   · HER READING is typed in a box under the drawing (a decimal keypad on a phone; "2½", "2 1/2" and "2.5" all read as 2½), kept on the
//     try without a repaint, and handed to the engine as the step's answer when it is within tol, else as she typed it — the engine's own
//     check then needs no change. Nothing on the drawing tells her the number before Check.
//   · THE DRAWING is inline SVG (no image file), made from the formulas: the trunk at breast height with the stick across it, zoomed so
//     the inch marks read at 430 px (each whole inch ticked, every inch or every other labeled by how close they sit); the tree from
//     66 ft with the stick upright beside it, the log marks every half log, the usable top dashed red. Phone-first at 430 px.
//   · WHICH STEP WAS MISSED: the ask's own id ("hold:height", "hold:reach", "stand") · "read:diameter" · "read:logs" · "read:feet" (she
//     typed the feet where the stick reads logs) · "math:partial" (left out the half log's 8 ft) · "math:feet". The Skills fold reads
